 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Internal network",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Each input has 50 kΩ to ground. A 15 kΩ resistor runs from the output back to pin 1; between pin 1 and pin 8 sits 1.35 kΩ, and from pin 8 a further 150 Ω leads to the inverting-side input stage, where the lower half of the bias divider also lands. Two 15 kΩ resistors in series from VS set the bias, with their junction brought out on pin 7 (Bypass)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gain setting",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Pins 1 and 8 open: the 1.35 kΩ resistor fixes the gain at 20 (26 dB). A capacitor between pins 1 and 8 bypasses that resistor and takes the gain to 200 (46 dB). Putting a resistor in series with that capacitor gives any gain between 20 and 200. A resistor or FET coupled through a capacitor from pin 1 to ground also controls the gain."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shaping the response",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Parts placed across the internal feedback resistors change gain and frequency response. A series RC from pin 1 to pin 5 parallels the internal 15 kΩ and lifts the bass: R = 15 kΩ gives about 6 dB of boost. With pin 8 open, R must stay at 10 kΩ or more for stable operation; with pins 1 and 8 bypassed it may go down to 2 kΩ. The limit exists because the amplifier is compensated only for closed-loop gains above 9."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input bias and offset",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Input transistor base current is about 250 nA, so an open input sits near 12.5 mV through its 50 kΩ to ground. A DC source resistance above 250 kΩ adds little extra offset (about 2.5 mV at the input, 50 mV at the output). With less than 10 kΩ of source resistance, shorting the unused input to ground holds the offset to about 2.5 mV at the input and 50 mV at the output. For source resistances in between, grounding the unused input through a resistor matching the source resistance cancels the excess offset. A capacitively coupled input has no offset problem."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused input at high gain",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "When pins 1 and 8 are bypassed for high gain, the unused input must be bypassed too, or gain drops and the amplifier may become unstable. Use 0.1 µF to ground, or a direct short, depending on the DC source resistance at the driven input."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output bias and quiescent power",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The output biases itself to half the supply voltage. Quiescent drain is 24 mW from a 6 V supply."
  }
 ]
}
